00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:03.975
We managed to
essentially work together

00:12:03.975 --> 00:12:05.260
to create the solution.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:09.370
And the technology we use is
the NSX SD-WAN by VeloCloud.

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:11.860
it's an SD-WAN technology,
that the way it works

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:16.488
is on the far left, we
have what we call a net.

00:12:16.488 --> 00:12:19.030
And this is a managed service
that essentially the only thing

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:23.090
you have to do is, you'll
get one of its appliances.

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:25.270
You're connected
to your location,

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:28.573
connects automatically to your
orchestrator, and that's it.

00:12:28.573 --> 00:12:29.990
Now you've go to
the orchestrator,

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:32.590
and you can manage your global
network of all the branches

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:35.360
that you have, as if
it was a single entity.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:39.110
And in there, you can set global
policies in terms of security,

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:41.948
in terms of connectivity,
in terms of application SLA.

00:12:41.948 --> 00:12:43.990
And the SD-WAN solution,
what it does essentially

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:47.140
is constantly monitors
the flow of data traffic

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:49.780
going from the edge,
to any other location.

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:52.660
In this case, going through
what we call a gateway,

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.090
a cloud gateway, the
green box in the middle,

00:12:55.090 --> 00:12:56.470
or to other edges.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:58.750
And now it provides
you a central view

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:01.150
of this square
problem that Ines was

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:04.000
mentioning before, end to end.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.100
And then you start
reasoning in terms of,

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:10.090
how do I provide an SLA
policy for a specific app that

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:13.180
needs to be guaranteed
regardless of what it talks to?

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:15.310
How do I provide
the virtual segment

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:16.978
that is going to
essentially regulate

00:13:16.978 --> 00:13:18.520
a component of the
traffic that needs

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:21.880
to talk to a critical part of
my applications in the back end,

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:24.430
or it needs to talk
across different branches?

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:26.483
How do I set up
security policies?

00:13:26.483 --> 00:13:28.150
But you do it at the
orchestrator level,

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:30.670
without having to worry
about the specifics

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:33.520
of the site, where you
may have to manage that.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:35.590
Now what we did with
Google, we started

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:39.280
working on how to connect the
SD-WAN technology by VMware

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:40.598
to the Google capabilities.

00:13:40.598 --> 00:13:42.640
And we are going to explain
it in the next slide,

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:44.057
but basically you
see that there's

00:13:44.057 --> 00:13:47.980
multiple ways to connect the
SD-WAN technology to the cloud.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:51.670
One is using the gateway
capabilities to VPN,

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.580
into native cloud services.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:55.810
And this is one integration
we've done with Google.

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:57.310
But there's other ways
that we are exploring,

00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:58.852
that are much more
efficient in terms

00:13:58.852 --> 00:14:02.890
of how to put a brand's
capability inside the cloud

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:06.010
itself, in a way that now you
have a native connectivity

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:09.190
to the VPC, or to the
element that you want

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.120
to have in the global cloud.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.600
So with this background,
what we did is

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:17.120
we started working with
Google towards connecting,

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:19.630
as I was mentioning,
all the sites.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:21.190
Regardless if they
were data centers

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:23.478
finding applications
or branches needing

00:14:23.478 --> 00:14:26.020
to connect to the applications
not only to the private cloud,

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:27.630
but to the public cloud.

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:30.570
And by federating with the
Google VPN capabilities,

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:33.010
now we provided a global
build with the NSX

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.980
as the one orchestrator,
to essentially manage

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:39.770
the connectivity as a
capability, as a service.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:42.370
So we went from having
to manage thousands

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:44.920
of VPN points, thousands
of firewall roles,

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:48.440
trying to understand in what
location the closest Google

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:51.220
service would be, to just
a simple tag that you say,

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:53.790
enable GCP connectivity.

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:54.988
And problem solved.

00:14:54.988 --> 00:14:57.280
And then of course, you can
go into a more granular way

00:14:57.280 --> 00:15:00.380
about defining application
SLAs and security policies.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:03.460
We are going to see a very
quick demo of this in a second,

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:06.750
but later we can
get into details

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:09.130
and explain you more
about how this works.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:10.870
So this was one of
the major problems

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:12.978
that Ines was mentioning.

00:15:12.978 --> 00:15:14.770
How to connect to the
Google Cloud services

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:16.420
with a single point
of management.

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:19.810
But more important was, cloud
is not in a static thing.

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:21.430
You keep deploying
more applications,

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:22.900
you have your CI/CD
pipelines, you

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:24.740
have your production systems.

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:26.410
You have your PCI
compliance systems.

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:27.910
You have different
environments that

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:29.158
constantly grow in strength.

00:15:29.158 --> 00:15:30.700
The nature of cloud
is that you don't

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:33.610
have a set of
predefined IP addresses,

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:35.337
or predefined resources.

00:15:35.337 --> 00:15:37.920
What you do is like essentially
you grow and shrink as needed.

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.950
You deploy more
environments as needed.

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:42.100
So the [INAUDIBLE],,
the operational aspect,

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:44.290
is essentially where the
significant advantage

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:46.000
towards customers were coming.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.040
It was not just how
to onboard the cloud.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:50.955
It's how to onboard the cloud,
and constantly maintain it,

00:15:50.955 --> 00:15:53.080
as applications were coming
and going, and growing,

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:54.740
and shrinking in the cloud.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.160
So this was the first
part of the collaboration.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:58.720
And after that, Ines, all
the problems we're solved,

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:00.015
and we could go home, right?

00:16:00.015 --> 00:16:00.932
SPEAKER 2: Yeah, yeah.

00:16:00.932 --> 00:16:02.250
We should all go home.

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:04.110
Yeah, not so fast yet.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:07.490
There are still
problems to be solved.

00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:13.390
So there are, of course,
you know the connectivity.

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:15.190
It is the connectivity
aspect of it,

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:20.140
on onboarding and maintaining,
as Perry was mentioning.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:25.930
But there are additional use
cases now, as enterprises.

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:30.370
And you guys are totally
familiar with this.

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:33.610
You onboard cloud,
and then you start

00:16:33.610 --> 00:16:35.920
deploying applications
and services

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:37.910
where there are opportunities.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:41.350
But there are reasons
why certain applications

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:45.280
and services need
to remain on prem,

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:48.490
and those will be because
practical reasons.

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.220
You know, it is not possible
to re-platform those,

00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:55.570
or it could be because of
regulatory reasons, or location

00:16:55.570 --> 00:17:00.160
reasons for latency needs,
that those services need

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:01.820
to be localized.

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:04.960
So what we see is
that really, this

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:09.010
creates cross dependencies
of applications

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:14.800
that need to stay on
prem, and then services

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.810
that will be onboarded in cloud.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:19.720
Another example
that is for example,

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:24.369
CI/CD deployments that
happen in cloud, in order

00:17:24.369 --> 00:17:26.093
to get advantage
of the flexibility

00:17:26.093 --> 00:17:27.010
of those environments.

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:32.192
But you want to deploy the
images to your applications

00:17:32.192 --> 00:17:33.400
that are running on premises.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:38.410
So all this really
creates really, truly,

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:43.930
a mesh of applications that need
to communicate, to be secure,

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:47.380
and to which we need
to really control

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:49.630
the communication,
the authentication,

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:52.510
and we need to get visibility
about the performance

00:17:52.510 --> 00:17:58.070
on the way these applications
talk to each other.

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:02.770
So in order to do
that, we have a bigger,

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:06.970
or another big problem, to
solve, which is the ability

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:09.820
to really federate
the communication

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:11.860
and the management
of these services,

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:15.780
in a way that is seamless
across both environments.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.370
And there is a
significant effort

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:21.940
that VMware and us
are driving together,

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:26.290
in which we have a common vision
on how we are going to federate

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:31.180
these services in a way that
can be operated in an easy

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:33.850
way, and a simplified way,
for our enterprise customers.

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:41.560
From Google perspective, if
you just came from the keynote,

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:49.080
you saw that we have announced
Google Cloud's Anthos in GA.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:52.230
I want to talk a little
bit about what we do,

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:56.430
in terms of modernizing and
building a Cloud Services

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:58.980
Platform at Google.

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:02.220
We obviously have had
a lot of experience

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:05.860
already in Kubernetes
environments.

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:08.930
We have had for years now.

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:11.700
They are Google
Kubernetes engine

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:15.480
on the cloud, which is a managed
platform, in which we actually

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:17.590
solve the problem
for developers.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:23.280
But we also solve the problem of
the platform operator, in which

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:26.960
we ought to upgrade the node,
we maintain, we are able scale,

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:27.990
we will repair.

00:19:27.990 --> 00:19:33.000
So we're really seeing our
value as not only solving

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:36.270
the developer problem, but also
the administrative platform

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:41.640
operator problem, and really
fully give a managed platform

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:43.050
for Kubernetes.

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:46.920
What we have done is we
have expanded to on premises

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:52.380
with GKE On-Prem, and we really
remain with the same values,

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:57.420
value prop, and providing a
managed platform for Kubernetes

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:00.780
in on prem, following the
same principles that we

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:01.720
had in cloud.

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:04.230
And on top of that,
we build value.

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:09.000
And we are providing
control plane capabilities

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:13.170
which go from cluster
registry and management,

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:14.910
being able to
authenticate the cluster,

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:18.130
so that we can actually
manage and operate it.

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:20.220
We provide visibility
with the integration

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:22.710
of the StackDriver with logs.

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:27.750
We are, as well, providing
config management capability,

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:33.630
so that the configuration of the
different services and clusters

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:38.370
can be done from a
centralized place.

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:43.810
We have built, as well,
a marketplace platform,

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:45.960
so that the
Kubernetes application

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:48.780
is not only for ours,
but for third parties can

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:51.870
be onboarded, and
consumed by our customers

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.530
in a seamless way.

00:20:53.530 --> 00:20:56.670
And finally, and this is
the part in which we're

00:20:56.670 --> 00:21:01.770
going to talk more today,
we have an Istio Service

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:07.110
Management that
we are providing,

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:12.180
for managing and operating the
service mix of the services.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:15.600
And in this specific
area is where

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:19.560
we are collaborating
very closely with VMware

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:22.020
to provide solution of
the operational management

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:23.280
of those services.

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:27.150
I want to just highlight that
really the vision that we have

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:29.770
is built on the open
source capabilities

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:33.210
at these various layers, like
it anchors on the Kubernetes

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:36.420
for orchestrating workloads,
it anchors in Istio

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:38.160
for the service management.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:41.940
And we really have a common
view of how we can actually

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:48.270
make those open source,
managed value, on both sides,

00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:50.610
work together
seamlessly to solve

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:52.990
the problem of the
service management.

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:55.910
And with that I
want to have Perry,

00:21:55.910 --> 00:21:58.500
as well, talk more
about how we're actually

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:01.488
federating both environments.

00:22:01.488 --> 00:22:03.030
SPEAKER 1: So as
Ines was mentioning,

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:05.820
what we started seeing is
that a lot of our customers

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:08.820
were starting to expand
the footprint where they

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:11.550
would develop those services.

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:13.950
By the foundation of Kubernetes,
we saw great traction

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:16.350
on how to create
microservices, and we

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:19.080
started seeing that because
of the footprint, the agility,

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:21.600
the ability to essentially
sunshade them, grow

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:23.400
them, shrink them,
we started to see

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:27.420
a proliferation of Kubernetes
environments in the enterprise.

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:31.410
Of course, we have the
PKS Kubernetes service

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:35.110
for the VC environments that we
are deploying in data centers.

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:38.670
But we started to see Kubernetes
appearing in the branches.

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:40.170
There are a lot of
retailers looking

00:22:40.170 --> 00:22:42.510
on how to modernize
the retail experience,

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:45.595
by putting digital signage,
points of presence,

00:22:45.595 --> 00:22:47.220
detection of who's
coming to the store,

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:49.650
and customizing the
behavior of the stores

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:50.800
based on who you are.

00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:53.970
So that requires some
lightweight form of compute,

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:55.188
that would be very agile.

00:22:55.188 --> 00:22:57.480
So this is where we started
seeing all these Kubernetes

00:22:57.480 --> 00:22:58.313
services everywhere.

00:22:58.313 --> 00:23:02.220
And now, as Ines was mentioning,
this is a different problem.

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:04.290
Now we are not talking
about connectivity

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:07.740
from the point of view of IP
connectivity application SLAs,

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:11.220
but now we are saying, why these
applications should be talking

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:13.345
to your back end database?

00:23:13.345 --> 00:23:15.220
Is this application a
production environment?

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:16.980
Is this a development
environment?

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:19.740
Is this application
connecting to the database

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:21.880
on behalf of a user?

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:24.120
So the problems went
a little bit higher,

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:26.430
where not we were not
talking about connectivity

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:28.800
from networking point of view.

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:31.970
But we started to see
the problems of identity,

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:35.910
the problems of understanding
what applications are doing

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:38.400
on behalf of the users
that they have connected

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:41.345
to the application, what
data elements will you touch?

00:23:41.345 --> 00:23:42.720
So as Ines was
saying, we started

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:44.790
to look at this problem
a couple of years ago.

00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:46.590
And we were saying,
OK, this is going

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:49.620
to be a new wave of things
that we have to work

00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:51.512
with enterprises to solve them.

00:23:51.512 --> 00:23:53.220
And we started to work
around the project

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:58.030
of vSphere and Envoy, in a
way to be able to solve that.

00:23:58.030 --> 00:23:59.570
But there was this
problem of, OK.

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:01.680
So I have multiple
Kubernetes environments.

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:05.333
I could create what's called
a service mesh, in each

00:24:05.333 --> 00:24:07.500
of those environments, where
now we have the ability

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:09.870
to connect a service
to another service,

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:12.060
and understand what
API calls, what

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:13.770
authentication
events, authorization,

00:24:13.770 --> 00:24:14.710
all these things.

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:18.077
But it would be confined
to every single location.

00:24:18.077 --> 00:24:19.410
And enterprises don't want that.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:22.230
They want to have, again,
a common way to understand

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:25.290
federated identity,
authentication authorization,

00:24:25.290 --> 00:24:26.670
data access.

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:30.260
So we had this gap in between
the multiple Kubernetes

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:31.218
environments.

00:24:31.218 --> 00:24:32.760
The service was
running in the cloud,

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:36.240
and we had to figure out how to
solve, and how to bridge that.

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:41.703
So within this, we started
working on this project, NSX

00:24:41.703 --> 00:24:44.610
Service Mesh, which is a
product that we are developing,

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:46.380
and now we are in
better state, but we

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:49.110
are going to announce it
soon as fully available.

00:24:49.110 --> 00:24:52.500
That essentially was the
ability to essentially control

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:55.590
what we call a data plane inside
the Kubernetes environment,

00:24:55.590 --> 00:24:59.310
based on Istio and Envoy,
and be able to control

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:03.600
through the service that we are
creating on top, many of them.

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.160
And the idea is that
from this vantage point,

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:08.550
we have the ability to
essentially discover

00:25:08.550 --> 00:25:11.310
all the services that are
running in the Kubernetes

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:12.690
environments.

00:25:12.690 --> 00:25:14.730
Then, once you discover
them, understand

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:15.960
how are they performing.

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:16.800
What are they doing?

00:25:16.800 --> 00:25:20.772
What set of [INAUDIBLE] and be
able to react based on that.

00:25:20.772 --> 00:25:22.980
After that, essentially
focus on how to control them.

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:24.840
How to put security policies?

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:27.320
How to put application
level policies?

00:25:27.320 --> 00:25:28.980
And getting into
the security aspect

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:31.680
that we are discussing, on
how to have an understanding

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:33.630
of security end to end.

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:35.640
We think this, Google
was working on answers,

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:37.930
and we are saying, well, I
mean we have common goals.

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:40.680
We are all trying to solve
the notion of how enterprises

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:45.030
are going to use services across
clouds in a centralized way,

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:48.360
that now identity, policy,
visibility, observability,

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:49.630
are going to be consistent.

00:25:49.630 --> 00:25:51.005
Because at the
end of the day, it

00:25:51.005 --> 00:25:52.485
is all about
operations, about how

00:25:52.485 --> 00:25:54.360
to keep your applications
running, regardless

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:55.720
of where they are.

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:57.818
So within this
environment, again, we

00:25:57.818 --> 00:25:59.110
went back to the drawing board.

00:25:59.110 --> 00:26:02.100
And we said, well
Google is providing

00:26:02.100 --> 00:26:03.990
Istio and Envoy
based capabilities

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:06.990
in their VK environment.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:09.120
We are working with our
Kubernetes environments

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:11.370
and providing
standard capabilities.

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:14.040
What if the control planes
that we have developed

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:16.890
would start to federate
between ourselves.

00:26:16.890 --> 00:26:19.530
In a way that now, from
a customer experience,

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:23.072
what you have essentially is an
end to an mesh of microservices

00:26:23.072 --> 00:26:24.780
talking to each other,
regardless if they

00:26:24.780 --> 00:26:27.360
are native in cloud,
in public cloud,

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:29.760
or running on your branches
or your private cloud.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:33.210
They all form part
of a entwined mesh.

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:35.820
Now of course, that's
easier said than done,

00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:38.820
because in reality there's many
more things needed in the sense

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:42.210
that we have to create the
ability to create, manage,

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:44.700
like domains or
global name spaces,

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:47.265
in a way that not all the
services talk to each other.

00:26:47.265 --> 00:26:49.140
You're going to have
production applications,

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:51.057
you're going to have
development environments,

00:26:51.057 --> 00:26:52.960
you're going to have
sensitive things.

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:55.585
So it's not just a
flat end to end mesh,

00:26:55.585 --> 00:26:57.210
but it's what we are
doing with Google,

00:26:57.210 --> 00:26:59.700
is bringing the capabilities
on top of the open source

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:01.470
components that
we are developing,

00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:04.520
about how to make it
work for enterprises.

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:06.520
How to provide the proper
level of segmentation.

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:08.730
How to provide the proper
level of visibility.

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:12.570
Essentially the NSX
service mesh control

00:27:12.570 --> 00:27:15.370
element, whether it's with
the Anthos controller in a way

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:17.940
that now we can have
a federated identity,

00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:20.280
that when a service
on prem talks

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:23.070
to a service in the cloud,
now we maintain the end

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:25.420
to end properties of
that communication,

00:27:25.420 --> 00:27:27.930
being able to have essentially
mutually authenticated TLS

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:31.060
sessions, with a proper level
of encryption authentication,

00:27:31.060 --> 00:27:32.620
authorization, and so on.

00:27:32.620 --> 00:27:34.710
So that was going up the stack.

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:36.960
How to go from the
connectivity plane

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:39.010
that we provide in
the previous example.

00:27:39.010 --> 00:27:41.070
And now go one level
above, and start

00:27:41.070 --> 00:27:43.890
to get into the notion
of, what are applications

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:45.870
doing, on behalf of whom?

00:27:45.870 --> 00:27:48.540
And what data elements
do you touch, end to end,

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:50.010
across multiple clouds?

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:52.080
So that's the nature
of the cooperation.

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:55.350
Of course we are going
through a journey,

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:57.780
and what we started
working on is essentially--

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:01.150
this is the high level
overview of the project.

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:04.230
So we have a Kubernetes
environments,

00:28:04.230 --> 00:28:08.370
VMware, PKS on prem,
controlled by service mesh.

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:11.610
And then we have GK clusters
running Kubernetes, and talking

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:15.430
to [INAUDIBLE] native services,
with Anthos controlling this.

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:17.650
And we started
essentially controlling

00:28:17.650 --> 00:28:19.680
the interoperability
of services talking

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:22.440
from private to public
cloud, and vise versa,

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:24.660
at the federation level
with end to end authority,

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:26.290
to authenticate
the TLS sessions.

00:28:26.290 --> 00:28:28.110
That's the part that
we are demonstrating.

00:28:28.110 --> 00:28:31.210
Right now, we are going
to show some little video.

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:34.260
And if you want more
details in the booth later.

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:35.850
But the work doesn't end here.

00:28:35.850 --> 00:28:38.910
Of course now, we have to go
to the next level of saying,

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:40.420
sure I could federate identity.

00:28:40.420 --> 00:28:42.600
I could federate end
to end encryption.

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:45.042
But what about
coordinating policies?

00:28:45.042 --> 00:28:47.250
Because now you're going to
have a set of hierarchies

00:28:47.250 --> 00:28:49.630
of policies, organizations,
groups, roles,

00:28:49.630 --> 00:28:51.030
and so on, on the cloud.

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:53.850
You're going to have
another one on the on prem.

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:56.920
Now the idea is, how to bring
all these things together?

00:28:56.920 --> 00:28:58.920
So take this collaboration
as the starting point

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.180
of solving this problem.

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:01.920
Still lots of work to do.

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:04.890
So we'll continue to work as
the project evolves with Google.

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:07.200
But the end goal
essentially is to allow end

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:10.050
to end visibility,
connectivity, security, policy

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:13.860
compliance, between public and
private, at the service level,

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:18.960
understanding from user,
services, and data, end to end.

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:21.510
So with this, maybe let's talk
a little bit about the work

00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:23.380
we did together.

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:24.300
SPEAKER 2: Yeah.

00:29:24.300 --> 00:29:28.830
So by the way, we're going
to show a quick demo video.

00:29:28.830 --> 00:29:32.640
Please stop by the booth,
really to get more information

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:34.440
and more details.

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:36.690
This is just a quick snapshot.

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:41.460
There is a lot more data that
can be made available to you

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:43.360
there.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:46.080
Just to quickly set the stage.

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:49.380
I just want to recap
about the challenges

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:52.350
that we're about to demonstrate.

00:29:52.350 --> 00:29:57.390
And just to really get a view
of what those challenges are,

00:29:57.390 --> 00:30:01.050
just imagine business
transformation

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:02.880
for our retailer, and
you know the journey

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:06.640
that they go through.

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:11.610
They might have checkout
payment, shipment services,

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:15.630
that it's monolithic,
running in on prem.

00:30:15.630 --> 00:30:18.720
And then they start moving
some of the capabilities

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:23.460
to cloud for the flexibility,
the pay as you grow,

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:26.200
different capabilities
of the cloud.

00:30:26.200 --> 00:30:28.160
So in this case,
they're moving catalog.

00:30:28.160 --> 00:30:29.790
So as you are
actually doing that,

00:30:29.790 --> 00:30:31.980
then you face the
problem of connectivity.

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:35.700
That's how we started, actually,
discussing in the session.

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:39.800
And you need to make sure that
your branches get connected

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:43.050
to the cloud, and you need
to connect those applications

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:44.410
on top of that.

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:48.083
And now it might be that you're
actually breaking the most

00:30:48.083 --> 00:30:49.500
monolithic, that
you have on prem,

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:52.560
and then you start
creating microservices

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:57.580
that are some pieces of the
monolithic that you had before.

00:30:57.580 --> 00:31:00.330
So for example, you're
breaking payments on shipments.

00:31:00.330 --> 00:31:02.550
But really all
these applications

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:04.270
need to communicate
with each other.

00:31:04.270 --> 00:31:05.380
There are dependencies.

00:31:05.380 --> 00:31:07.830
There's data that they
need to share, that state,

00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:12.420
or there is some
type of communication

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:14.310
that needs to happen.

00:31:14.310 --> 00:31:16.590
You still need to have the
communication to the cloud,

00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:19.170
and then you add complexity
with the communication

00:31:19.170 --> 00:31:23.070
of the applications on top
of it, on prem and in cloud.

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:25.770
And as you go through
the transformation,

00:31:25.770 --> 00:31:29.100
you see opportunities
for getting

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:30.692
more insight of your data.

00:31:30.692 --> 00:31:32.400
For example, in your
branches, and you'll

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:36.480
see an opportunity
of having data image

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:38.520
recognition in the branches.

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:41.640
And for example,
having ML train models

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:43.500
that you are running
in cloud, in which you

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:47.370
can extract that meaningful
information, real time,

00:31:47.370 --> 00:31:48.300
in your branches.

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:49.780
What that requires
is, of course,

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:51.060
the underlying connectivity.

00:31:51.060 --> 00:31:53.980
But it also creates
additional dependency,

00:31:53.980 --> 00:31:57.810
so your communication of
applications from on services,

00:31:57.810 --> 00:32:01.680
and to cloud, and so
on, keeps growing.

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:04.800
You then add
recommendation or you

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:08.430
want to do ad insertion to
add value to your business.

00:32:08.430 --> 00:32:11.130
And all that, really
what it creates,

00:32:11.130 --> 00:32:12.730
and this is just a sample.

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:16.230
It is a very
complicated relationship

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:19.290
of services that have
dependencies among each other.

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:22.830
So the key is how do
you secure, operate?

00:32:22.830 --> 00:32:25.710
How in this environment you
make sure that, for example,

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:28.590
your dev environments are not
talking to your production

00:32:28.590 --> 00:32:31.800
environments, that there
is no data that is crossing

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:34.920
boundaries, that is not safe?

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:38.280
The servability and security
of these deployments

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:43.260
is challenging, unless you
have a way of actually having

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:47.460
a single view of these
in a single operation

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:49.240
across all these deployments.

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:52.900
And this is really what we want
to talk about in the video.

00:32:52.900 --> 00:33:01.830
So with that, just want
to do a quick show.

